From 31a9b74b619fa7cf40d588a14ff18b59bed28f57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 05:47:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Stream the answer instead of making the trainer wait for
 all of it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Production logs recorded client_agent_answered with latency_ms 48155 and
57022 — successes, no exception — while the browser had already shown an
error. A non-streaming turn is an idle connection for its entire life, and
something between the two gives up at about sixty seconds. Raising timeouts
only moves that wall; a stream removes it, and the trainer starts reading at
the first sentence rather than the last.

POST /ai/client-agent/chat/stream emits the same envelope as /chat over SSE,
in the shape the frontend's ai-stream.ts already parses.

Two things are easy to get wrong once a response becomes a stream, so both
are pinned by tests and mutation-checked:

  - The status code is spent on the first byte. Authorisation and retrieval
    complete before anything is written, so a client the caller may not see
    still gets a 404 rather than a 200 with a denial inside. The response
    then holds its status line back a further two seconds, because the
    failures that actually happen — a withdrawn model 404ing, a free-tier
    quota 429ing — happen instantly and deserve a real status. Past that
    point the stream opens on the timer, since silence is what proxies
    punish.
  - A retry is no longer free. Falling back to a second model after tokens
    are on screen splices one answer's opening onto another's middle, so
    fallback is allowed only before the first token; after it, a failure is
    reported as a failure, with partial telling the UI whether the text on
    screen is a truncated answer or nothing at all.

The provider's stream timeout is rolling rather than fixed: it should catch
a model that has stopped producing, not one producing steadily but slowly.

ask() and askStream() share prepare(), so the authorisation step cannot be
fixed in one path and left broken in the path that reaches client data.
